"Iran-backed Houthi rebels take strategic port city in Yemen, raising threat to Red Sea shipping (https://apnews.com/article/e4e799701b382799a955969c212800ea)\nciteturn1news12 [wordlim: 100] Published: today; Date: Thu Sep 10 14:04:28 2026 UTC ... The Houthis kept mostly to the sidelines when the Iran war began, but over the summer the rebels increased attacks against Yemen's internationally recognized government and declared a blockade on Saudi Arabia, which supports it. ... It briefly increased this year as Saudi Arabia sought alternatives to Hormuz; Saudi oil shipments were redirected via pipeline to the Red Sea port at Yanbu, but \"renewed Houthi threats have curtailed\" that activity, according to Lloyd's List Intelligence.\nIran-backed Houthi rebels take strategic port city in Yemen, raising threat to Red Sea shipping\nDate: Thu Sep 10 14:04:28 2026 UTC\nLocation: Aden, Yemen\n\nADEN, Yemen (AP) - The Iran-backed Houthi rebel group on Thursday took over Yemen's port city of Mokha, an area vital to controlling shipping through the Red Sea, Houthi and Yemeni officials said.\nThe takeover gives Iran another potential point of leverage in its war with the U.S., analysts said, if the Houthis use their presence in Mokha to ramp up threats or attacks on shipping in the Red Sea.\nMokha is about 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the Bab el-Mandeb Strait, a waterway that connects the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden and through which about 12% of the world's goods are typically shipped.\nThe Bab el-Mandeb's importance to global oil supplies has grown since shipping through the nearby Strait of Hormuz has been impeded by Iran during its war with the United States and Israel. \nThe Houthis kept mostly to the sidelines when the Iran war began, but over the summer the rebels increased attacks against Yemen's internationally recognized government and declared a blockade on Saudi Arabia, which supports it. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHouthis seize strategic Red Sea port in Yemen, and other Mideast developments (https://apnews.com/article/4de45665de2221865501a4c512daa86d)\nciteturn1news13 [wordlim: 100] Published: today; Houthis seize strategic Red Sea port in Yemen, and other Mideast developmentsDate: Thu Sep 10 06:48:28 2026 UTC ... Neighboring Saudi Arabia said it would take all measures necessary to deter Houthi attacks.\nHouthis seize strategic Red Sea port in Yemen, and other Mideast developments\nDate: Thu Sep 10 06:48:28 2026 UTC\n\nIranian-backed Houthi rebels seized Yemen's strategic Red Sea port city of Mokha, according to Houthi and Yemeni officials, bringing the rebels closer to controlling a critical waterway to the Red Sea. \nSeparately, an Israeli strike killed at least four people in northern Gaza, including two children, hospital officials said Thursday. The Israeli military said the attack killed a militant.\nHere's a look at the latest news in the Middle East. Full coverage can be found here.\n\nYemen's Houthi rebels take key port city, officials say\n\nThe Iran-backed Houthi rebel group on Thursday took over Yemen's port city of Mokha, Houthi and Yemeni officials said.\nMokha is about 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the Bab el-Mandeb Strait, a strategic waterway between the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden through which about 12% of the world's oil and other goods are shipped.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nYemen risks a return to civil war as Iran-backed rebels target a vital oil shipping route (https://apnews.com/article/f9cb75eca9a89664db0b6e84e4161ea0)\nciteturn1news14 [wordlim: 100] Published: today; Date: Thu Sep 10 10:16:38 2026 UTC ... Now, with the Iran war choking the Strait of Hormuz, the Houthis have a new way to inflict pain on their neighbor by declaring a blockade against Saudi Arabia and targeting its efforts to ship its oil via the Red Sea. ... Now, the Houthis are back with a different target, aiming